    "narrative": "The lineup for July 20 is anchored by **Geraldo Perdomo** (73.81) and **Cal Raleigh** (71.46), who stand as our premier plays due to favorable matchups against Jeffrey Springs and Andrew Abbott. **Geraldo Perdomo** benefits immensely from a projected #2 spot against the lefty Springs, with his score bolstered by a 16% SP skill advantage. Meanwhile, **Cal Raleigh** remains a high-floor start despite a mediocre park factor; his ability to leverage the platoon advantage against Abbott provides the necessary boost to remain well within our \"Strong\" efficiency tier. Similarly, **Daulton Varsho** (69.94) and **Luis Arraez** (67.03) provide excellent value, with **Luis Arraez** offering a high-contact floor out of the leadoff spot against Michael Wacha, mitigating the risk of the higher-tier opposition.\n\nRegarding the bench, the decision to exclude **Masyn Winn** (52.13) was the most difficult. While he offers legitimate stolen base potential\u2014a high priority given our league context\u2014he was edged out by the superior platoon and park dynamics of **Tyler Freeman** and **Lawrence Butler**. **Masyn Winn** is suppressed by the risk of facing a high-efficiency catcher (1.89s pop time) and the defensive profile of Jos\u00e9 Soriano, which lowered his projected SB ceiling enough for the optimizer to favor the power-oriented potential of **Lawrence Butler** against Mitch Bratt\u2019s poor 7.04 xERA. \n\nWe are monitoring the \"Lineup Pending\" status for all starters, particularly **Yainer Diaz** and **Gavin Sheets**, as shifts in the batting order could significantly impact their daily efficiency scores. **Bryce Harper** remains a lock despite facing Emmet Sheehan\u2019s 3.67 xERA; his elite xwOBA (0.389) serves as the buffer necessary to overcome a tougher matchup. Owners should be wary of the volatility surrounding **Lawrence Butler**; while the matchup against the lefty Bratt is favorable on paper, his scoring is heavily reliant on avoiding early-game substitutions if the game script turns.",
